  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **dot product** of two vectors multiplies matching components and adds the results. The answer is a plain number, not a vector.",
    "facts": [
"In two dimensions, $\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b}=a_{1}b_{1}+a_{2}b_{2}$.",
"In three dimensions, add the third product: $\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b}=a_{1}b_{1}+a_{2}b_{2}+a_{3}b_{3}$.",
"The dot product is a **scalar**, so the answer carries no $\\underset{\\sim}{i}$ or $\\underset{\\sim}{j}$.",
"A missing component counts as $0$, so $7\\underset{\\sim}{i}$ means $a_{2}=0$.",
"The order does not matter: $\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b}=\\underset{\\sim}{b}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{a}$.",
"The dot product is distributive: $\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot(\\underset{\\sim}{b}+\\underset{\\sim}{c})=\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b}+\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{c}$.",
"A scalar factor comes out the front: $(k\\underset{\\sim}{a})\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b}=k(\\underset{\\sim}{a}\\cdot\\underset{\\sim}{b})$.",
"A negative dot product means the angle between the non-zero vectors is obtuse, or $180^\\circ$ if they point in exactly opposite directions."
],
    "steps": [
      "Multiply the matching components",
      "Add the products"
    ]
  },
